#!/bin/sh
#
# Runs the quality gate before every commit. Versioned here rather than in
# .git/hooks/, which Git does not track: activated with
#
#   git config core.hooksPath .githooks
#
# which `npm install` also does through the "prepare" script.
#
# The gate is four checks, in the order they refuse:
#
#   1. types      tsc over src/ *including* the test files, which the
#                 publishing config excludes. It also emits the JavaScript
#                 the suite then runs, so the check and the run are the same
#                 artefact and cannot describe different code.
#   2. suite      every test, run against that output. The same run collects
#                 which scenario each test declares, and measures which lines,
#                 branches and functions were reached.
#   3. scenarios  every scenario in openspec/specs/ is either covered by a
#                 test or declared out of scope with a reason, and nothing
#                 that openspec/coverage.json records as covered has stopped
#                 being covered. This comes before the code floor because it
#                 names which promise is undefended, which is more actionable
#                 than naming a line.
#   4. code       every line, branch and function of production code is
#                 exercised, or is marked as deliberately not exercised with
#                 a `// Coverage reason:` beside the directive.
#
# Nothing here is a feature of opsx-tools. This is how this repository holds
# itself to its own specs: no component provisions it, no subcommand runs it,
# and none of it is written into a user's project.
#
# `git commit --no-verify` skips all of it, deliberately and knowingly. That is
# not a hole in the gate — it is the gate being honest about what it is. A
# local hook can always be bypassed, and one that pretended otherwise would
# only invite being turned off for good. Bypassing is a choice the person
# making it owns; the gate's job is to make sure it is a choice and not an
# accident.

set -e

npm run --silent gate
